# ulmo 0.11.2

This release replaces the installed shell and Perl wrappers with native
Oberon tools, using the Ulm library for files, streams, arguments and
process execution.

## Native Oberon driver and runtime helpers

- `ulmo` is now a native Oberon executable. It handles command-line
  options, main-module inference, imported implementations, object
  conversion and linking.
- `genobrts` generates startup assembly in Oberon.
- `oblink` invokes the assembler and linker directly and selects the
  native timezone provider from the linked module dependencies.
- External tools are executed through `SysProcess.Fork`, `Exec` and
  `WaitFor`, with argument vectors rather than shell command strings.
- Timezone test fixtures are also generated by an Oberon program, so
  the normal build and regression checks no longer require Perl.

The familiar command-line forms remain available:

```sh
ulmo -m Hello.om
ulmo -m Hello.om o.om
ulmo -m Hello.om -o hello
ulmo -m Hello Hello.om
```

Compiler processes remain separate and sequential, retaining the existing
memory-conscious build model.

## Dependencies and installation

Normal compilation does not invoke a shell or Perl interpreter. GNU `as`
and `ld` remain the assembler and linker. The ELF converter remains the
existing small C tool linked against libelf; no additional dependencies
have been introduced.

GNU make still uses a POSIX shell for its build recipes. Optional legacy
database tools and their older scripts remain in the source tree.

`/usr/bin/ulmo` is a symlink to the native driver under the configured
library directory, for example `/usr/lib64/ulmo/amd64/ulmo`. Private tools
and compiled interfaces are installed beside it.

`LIBDIR/ulmo/ulmo.sources` records the source directory. The driver locates
its tools through its executable path; `ULMOLIBDIR` and `ULMOSRCDIR`
remain available for overrides and staged installation tests.

Separate native link helpers are included in the AMD64 and i386 bootstrap
directories. The compiler, driver and runtime helpers are rebuilt during
the normal staged build.

## Verification

On both AMD64 and i386:

- `make check check-runtime check-cli` passed.
- The stage-2 and stage-3 versions of all five tools (`ulmoc`, `obtofgen`,
  `ulmo`, `genobrts` and `oblink`) are identical.
- All 13 runtime regression programs and the command-line checks passed.
- Native startup generation produced byte-identical assembler objects
  to the earlier Perl generators, with and without timezone startup.

The staged native installation passed command-line tests. An execution
trace confirmed that compilation invokes native tools, `as` and `ld`,
without shell or Perl wrappers.

The Gentoo ebuild drops Perl from build-time and runtime dependencies and
includes the native executables in its compiler-flags QA declarations.

Full changes: https://github.com/norayr/ulmo/compare/v0.11.1...v0.11.2
